\title{The gap to gruszka on Mathlib: about twice its warm wall clock}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{performance}
\tag{machine}
\tag{benchmark}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{origin}{docs/BENCHMARKS.md}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open, and much smaller than its title. Revised on 2026-11-24: "about twice" is the state of the afternoon. With the default configuration of the evening, the machine as default engine with eager reduction and the two proof-comparison shortcuts in its default set, the released executable is at 1.09 times gruszka's warm wall clock on Mathlib and the same configuration on the JVM launcher at 0.88 times. The first two parts of the list below are done; the last two are what remains.}
\p{\strong{The number at the end of 2026-11-24.} Warm, with 8 workers and loading pipelined with checking, on the 8 measurement cores of the aarch64 development host, from [the benchmark tables](rep-benchmarks) of the released jars: Mathlib takes the executable in its default configuration 15.46 s per pass, the same configuration on the JVM launcher 12.44 s, and gruszka with 8 threads 14.20 s on the same cores in a window of its own. In CPU time of the warm pass it is 118.1 s and 94.0 s against 79.9 s. On con-leche, navier-stokes-euler and cslib the executable is at 1.35, 1.37 and 1.24 times gruszka, on Init, Std and cedar at 1.00, 0.95 and 1.05, on reachability at 0.23. In instructions of a cold process, the arena's metric, Mathlib is 1,652.9 G against 418.7 G, 3.95 times. Nothing was measured on x86-64.}
\p{\strong{The number in the afternoon, as first written.} Warm, with 8 workers and loading pipelined with checking, on the 8 quiet performance cores of the aarch64 development host, Mathlib takes the machine 25.6 s on the JVM launcher with the switch set \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~} against 12.5 s for gruszka with 8 threads on the same cores: 2.05 times. With the larger default switch set of the same evening it is 21.23 s on the JVM against 12.32 s, 1.72 times, and 27.43 s in the native image, 2.23 times. In CPU time of the warm pass the first table has 188 s against 75.6 s. On Init, Std and cedar the machine is within a quarter to a half of gruszka, and it is faster on reachability. Source: the benchmark tables as they were that afternoon, section "Warm wall clock"; see [the warm table](ins-675U).}
\p{\strong{What is known.} The distance has four parts, by size.}
\ol{
\li{[The official way of comparing proofs](ins-HPBW): 40 percent of the instructions of the machine on Mathlib. The two shortcuts that remove it take the native image with 8 workers from 25.39 s to 19.15 s per warm pass. [No official-exact route](ins-FIXO) recovers it. The shortcuts are admissible under the practical policy; they cost on con-leche. \strong{Done:} both are in the default set since the evening of 2026-11-24, with a cap that removes the cost on con-leche, see [the decision](dec-YP0N).}
\li{[The units that enter eager reduction](ins-47IQ): about a tenth of the worker time, at the speed of the reference engine. The switch that keeps them on the machine exists and was off, see [eager reduction on the machine](prob-8MJ2). \strong{Done:} it is in the default set since the same evening.}
\li{[The cost of one machine operation](prob-28KQ): what remains after both is uniform, 3.9 times gruszka in instructions on Mathlib.}
\li{The parts that are not the engine: the loader thread, 13.5 s of a 25.6 s pass, right behind the checking, see [the loader as the floor](prob-I7M9); and in the native image the collector, see [garbage and the collector](prob-BIN4).}
}
\p{\strong{What would close it.} As first written: a decision, by warm wall clock over all corpora, whether the shortcuts are on by default; the switch \code{\verb~|eager~} reviewed and on; and then the cost per operation, which is the only part that also shows on the corpora where the machine is already close. No single item reaches gruszka: with the shortcuts on all of Mathlib the instruction ratio is still 3.88. The first two are done and took the JVM launcher past gruszka's wall clock on Mathlib. What is left for the executable is the cost per operation, 3.95 times in instructions and 1.48 times in CPU time of the warm pass, and the distance between the native image and the JVM launcher, 15.46 against 12.44 s; see also [what the release leaves open](prob-HC9J).}
\p{See also: [the decision for warm wall clock](dec-7QMU). Source of the revision: [the finalization session](ses-A14B).}
